<subsection xml:id="subsec-literature-gap">
  <title>Related Work and Methodological Gap</title>

  <!-- Issue 1: the field's raw analytical material is sequences, not states -->
  <p>
    Process data from digital assessments have moved from curiosity to
    infrastructure.  Large-scale programs such as PISA, TIMSS, and NAEP now
    release log files routinely, and recent reviews document a literature
    spanning international assessments, games, simulations, and
    course platforms (<xref ref="bib-anghel-2024"/>;
    <xref ref="bib-log-review-2025"/>).  Bergner and von Davier order its
    uses in a five-level hierarchy, from ignoring the process entirely to
    modeling the process itself as the outcome
    (<xref ref="bib-bergner-vondavier-2019"/>).
    Across all levels, however, the raw analytical material is almost always
    the recorded action sequence or indicators computed from it.  The complete
    state of a response between submissions is seldom an explicit analytic
    object, and even the definition of process data remains unsettled
    (<xref ref="bib-anghel-2024"/>).
  </p>

  <!-- Issue 2: sequence and feature methods presuppose defined objects -->
  <p>
    The methodological traditions that analyze these records generally begin
    after the analytical objects have been defined.  Educational process
    mining discovers workflow models from event logs and validates them by
    conformance checking, agreement between a discovered model and the log,
    while inheriting the log's case and activity definitions
    (<xref ref="bib-trcka-2010"/>; <xref ref="bib-van-der-aalst-2016"/>;
    <xref ref="bib-bogarin-2018"/>).  Psychometric feature extraction
    compresses given action sequences into n-gram patterns associated with success and failure
    (<xref ref="bib-he-vondavier-2016"/>), event-history representations
    (<xref ref="bib-chen-2019"/>), and generic embeddings whose
    quality is judged by reconstruction and prediction accuracy (<xref ref="bib-tang-mds-2020"/>;
    <xref ref="bib-tang-ae-2021"/>).  In both traditions, the mapping from
    platform rows to well-formed events, states, and analysis units is a
    precondition of the method, not an object of study.
  </p>

  <!-- Issue 3: reconstruction frameworks, the closest precedents -->
  <p>
    A smaller body of work treats that mapping itself as the methodological
    problem.  Kroehne and Goldhammer propose finite state machines whose
    states are theoretically defined phases of the response process,
    identified from platform-specific log events, separating event decoding
    from indicator definitions; they also define completeness conditions and
    observe that, when all answer changes are logged, the response can be
    re-derived and compared with the independently stored
    response as a verification device
    (<xref ref="bib-kroehne-goldhammer-2018"/>).  Keystroke-logging research
    offers a cross-domain parallel: logging programs register every input
    event so that the evolving text can be reconstructed and replayed
    (<xref ref="bib-leijten-vanwaes-2013"/>).  These frameworks establish the
    feasibility of reconstruction and articulate verification in principle.
    Their states, however, are coarse process phases rather than complete
    item configurations; the verification idea is stated as a data-quality
    condition rather than implemented as a quantified validation study; and
    neither aligns reconstructed states with authored item semantics or
    attaches an explicit readiness policy to the result.
  </p>

  <!-- Issue 4: validity requirements and preprocessing sensitivity -->
  <p>
    Two further strands sharpen the requirements such a transformation must
    meet.  Assessment validity theory names response processes among the
    recognized sources of validity evidence (<xref ref="bib-aera-2014"/>), and
    recent treatments argue that inferences from log-derived indicators
    require explicit theoretical and empirical justification
    (<xref ref="bib-ercikan-pellegrino-2017"/>;
    <xref ref="bib-zumbo-hubley-2017"/>;
    <xref ref="bib-goldhammer-zehner-2017"/>).  This program concentrates on
    the explanation inference, whether indicators reflect the intended
    construct; the prior scoring inference, that the identification rules
    extract the targeted behavior correctly, remains an assumption
    (<xref ref="bib-goldhammer-2021"/>).  At the same time, studies of
    preprocessing sensitivity show that justification cannot stop at the
    indicator: defensible alternative time-on-task estimation rules change
    downstream findings (<xref ref="bib-kovanovic-2015"/>), and reasonable
    alternative cleanings of the same course logs produce materially different
    results (<xref ref="bib-knobbout-2019"/>).  Multiverse analysis
    generalizes the remedy, reporting results across the datasets that
    reasonable processing choices generate and identifying the choices
    conclusions hinge on (<xref ref="bib-steegen-2016"/>).  Read together,
    these strands imply that reconstruction and segmentation belong to the
    measurement model and should be validated and systematically varied, not
    merely performed.
  </p>

  <!-- Issue 5: the interaction family has moved close to, but not onto, that ground -->
  <p>
    Studies of the relevant interaction family have moved close to, but not
    onto, that ground.  Analyses of NAEP drag-and-drop items derive strategy
    classifications, answer-change patterns, and time measures from action
    sequences, with cleaning rules (winsorization, first-action filtering)
    that are reported but neither varied nor validated
    (<xref ref="bib-jiang-2021"/>).  Gong and colleagues go furthest: they
    transcribe action sequences into sequences of complete target
    configurations to visualize answer formulation step by step, yet describe
    the transcription as heuristic, prune infrequent transitions for display,
    and check the result against no independent record
    (<xref ref="bib-gong-2022"/>).  Experimental work examines how
    drag-and-drop design features shape performance and response behavior
    (<xref ref="bib-arslan-emip"/>; <xref ref="bib-ponce-2021"/>).
    Sorting-based assessments score final sorts only
    (<xref ref="bib-smith-2013"/>; <xref ref="bib-tpl-kats"/>).  For Parsons
    problems, the nearest construction-task sibling, interaction traces
    reveal wide variation in solution paths
    (<xref ref="bib-helminen-2012"/>), and large-scale log analyses evaluate
    adaptation with outcome and count features
    (<xref ref="bib-ericson-2018"/>).  Across this family, reconstructed
    intermediate states are absent, implicit, or heuristic, and never
    validated against independently recorded configurations.
  </p>

  <!-- Two-sentence gap statement (checklist item 9) -->
  <p>
    In sum, sequence, process-mining, and feature-extraction methods
    presuppose that event types, response states, and analysis units have
    already been correctly derived; the closest reconstruction frameworks
    articulate completeness and verification in principle without
    implementing them as validation studies; and drag-and-drop, sorting, and
    Parsons-problem studies analyze action sequences, heuristically
    transcribed states, or final configurations that are never checked
    against independent records.  Source-dependent card-to-zone logs, in
    which moves arrive incrementally, complete configurations are recorded
    only at checks, and item semantics reside in the authored source,
    therefore require what this article supplies: an explicit,
    source-aligned, empirically validated, and sensitivity-tested
    transformation from platform rows to complete response trajectories.
  </p>
</subsection>

